MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three items of the 2026-08-30 text revision:

- Declaration clause order is free (keyword scheme rule 9): 'where', 'performs' and 'throws' are
  keyword-led and each appears at most once, so a fixed order buys nothing while 'where' already
  sits anywhere. Functional types keep the opposite rule -- there the pre-arrow clause sequence is
  one grammar production with 'performs' first -- and that check lives where such a type is parsed.

- The escape hatch is 'unsafeCanThrow', named for the capability it fabricates; 'unsafeAssumeHandled'
  was rejected as kind-ambiguous once the effect twin was specified.

- 'resume throwing e' is discharged by the catch clauses of its own 'try' (rule 5 as revised): the
  injected exception surfaces at the suspended 'perform', which is inside the try's body, so the
  text's own positive example must compile -- it did not. Implemented as a resume-only supply
  stack consulted by 'ResumeExpr' alone: an ordinary 'throw' in a handler body still resolves in
  the enclosing scopes, since the handler runs where it was installed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
